----------- ROUTING ------------
Routing means deciding what should happen when a user/client requests a particular URL and HTTP method.
          
app.get("/home", (req, res) => {
    res.send("Welcome Home");
});
      ->   GET → HTTP method
      ->   /home → route/path
      ->   Function → what to do when /home is requested
      
---> why we use Routing:
     it is used to create different URL's for different functionalities of our application.
     ex: /       ->Home
         /about  -> About page
         /products ->Products
         
----> When do we use Routing?
      We use routing whenever our application needs to respond differently to different requests.

express.Router() allows us to create separate, modular routes and then connect them to our main Express application.

Instead of putting every route inside app.js, we can divide them into separate files.

Why do we use Express Router?
Main reasons:
Organization - We can keep user routes separate from product routes.
Modularity

Each route file handles a particular functionality.

Maintainability - Large applications become easier to understand and modify.

Reusability - A router can be mounted under a particular path.




------- Cookies ---------
A cookie is a small piece of data that a website stores in the user's browser.
It is mainly used to remember information between requests.

> create cookie

app.get("/setcookie", (req, res) => {
    res.cookie("username", "Akshaya");
    res.send("Cookie set");
});

> cookie parse -- To read it,
const cookieParser = require("cookie-parser");

app.use(cookieParser());

app.get("/readcookie", (req, res) => {
    console.log(req.cookies.username);
    res.send("Cookie read");
});


>>> What is signed cookie
A signed cookie is a cookie that has an additional signature generated using a secret key.
Its purpose is to detect whether the cookie has been modified/tampered with by the user.
Signed cookies protect data from tampering, but they do not hide or encrypt the data.


